```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7,
                      fig.height = 5, fig.align = "center",
                      fig.path = "figures/tabicl2-benchmark-", error = FALSE)
library(VIM)
source("../dev-notes/benchmarks/tabicl2.R")
stopifnot(requireNamespace("ggplot2", quietly = TRUE))
```

```{r benchmark, include=FALSE}
config <- uci_config()
config$n <- c(250L, 500L, 1000L, 2000L)
config$repetitions <- 20L
result_dir <- Sys.getenv("VIM_TABICL2_RESULTS", "../.local/benchmarks/tabicl2-20reps")
cache_dir <- Sys.getenv("VIM_TABICL2_DATA", "../.local/benchmarks/tabicl2-cache")
# The local checkpoint cache resumes matching completed scenarios. The generated
# .Rmd embeds the outputs, so normal rendering never executes the benchmark.
# Explicit report-only mode never initializes Python or resumes model fitting.
# It may display an old protocol, but labels it as superseded below.
if (identical(Sys.getenv("VIM_TABICL2_CACHED_ONLY"), "true")) {
  config <- readRDS(file.path(result_dir, "config.rds"))
} else {
  reticulate::import("torch")$set_num_threads(1L)
  uci_run(config, cache = cache_dir, output = result_dir, download = TRUE,
          resume = file.exists(file.path(result_dir, "config.rds")))
}
metrics <- read.csv(file.path(result_dir, "metrics.csv"))
runs <- read.csv(file.path(result_dir, "runs.csv"))
stopifnot(all(runs$status == "ok"))
multi_rate_methods <- config$methods
have_results <- TRUE
has_ggplot <- TRUE
# Retain compact provenance alongside the frozen report; raw records stay local.
bundled <- "../dev-notes/benchmarks/tabicl2-results"
dir.create(bundled, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
for (f in c("config.rds", "runs.csv", "metrics.csv", "distributions.csv",
            "preprocessing.csv", "sources.csv", "session.txt"))
  stopifnot(file.copy(file.path(result_dir, f), file.path(bundled, f), overwrite = TRUE))
```

```{r protocol-status, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}
if (config$repetitions != 20L) {
  cat("> **Preliminary results:** This saved report contains ", config$repetitions,
      " repetitions. The requested 20-repetition experiment is not represented here yet.\n\n", sep = "")
}
if (!identical(config$methods, uci_config()$methods)) {
  cat("> **Different method set:** This cache does not contain the current ",
      "12-method comparison. Refresh the missing comparators before interpreting ",
      "it as the updated benchmark.\n\n")
}
if (!identical(config$protocol, uci_config()$protocol)) {
  cat("> **Superseded results:** These cached results predate the TabICL predictor ",
      "preprocessing fixes (original features, consistent missing-value handling ",
      "and typed categorical inputs). The TabICL comparisons below require a fresh run ",
      "and should not be interpreted as a comparison of the corrected variants.\n\n")
}
```

<style>
table { display: block; max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; }
</style>

## Introduction

This benchmark compares 12 imputation methods on numeric and categorical targets
in the Adult and Bank datasets, using shared samples and missingness masks.
The experiment uses 20 repetitions for each dataset, row count, missingness
mechanism and missingness rate. Results are evaluated on deliberately hidden
cells, whose true values are reserved for scoring.

| Method | Short explanation |
|:--|:--|
| `kNN` | Five nearest neighbours using equally weighted Gower distances; numeric medians and categorical modes. |
| `kNN_rf` | The same five-neighbour imputation with target-specific random-forest importance weights for the distances. |
| `cellwise` | Cell-weighted MM regression for numeric targets and weighted multinomial classification for categorical targets, in one pass. |
| `cellwise_seq10` | The same cellwise learners with up to ten sequential imputation passes. |
| `ranger` | Random-forest imputation with 100 trees, predicting each target from the original incomplete table. |
| `ranger_seq10` | Random-forest imputation with up to ten sequential passes. |
| `xgboost` | Gradient-boosted trees with 30 boosting rounds, predicting each target from the original incomplete table. |
| `xgboost_seq10` | Gradient-boosted tree imputation with up to ten sequential passes. |
| `vimpute_tabicl2` | Pretrained TabICL2 regression or classification with eight inference ensemble members, in one pass. |
| `vimpute_tabicl2_finetune` | TabICL2 fine-tuned on observed target values for up to five epochs, in one pass. |
| `vimpute_tabicl2_seq10` | Pretrained TabICL2 with up to ten sequential imputation passes. |
| `vimpute_tabicl2_finetune_seq10` | TabICL2 fine-tuned afresh at each fitted iteration, with up to ten sequential passes. |

This document is precomputed from its `.Rmd.orig` source. The generated `.Rmd`
contains static tables and figures, so rendering the saved report does not
initialize Python or fit models.

## Models and regeneration

All four TabICL variants call `vimpute(method = "tabicl2")`, with PMM and
residual noise disabled. Numeric targets use TabICLRegressor and factors use
TabICLClassifier. Fine-tuning uses the corresponding Finetuned estimators.
Each target and iteration fits a fresh model on observed target values only.

| Method | Fine-tuning | Sequential passes |
|:--|:--|:--|
| vimpute_tabicl2 | No | 1 |
| vimpute_tabicl2_finetune | Yes | 1 |
| vimpute_tabicl2_seq10 | No | Up to 10 |
| vimpute_tabicl2_finetune_seq10 | Yes | Up to 10 |

All variants use eight inference ensemble members on CPU. Fine-tuning uses
five epochs, learning rate `1e-5`, patience three, and two ensemble members
for training and validation, with mixed precision disabled. The upstream
internal validation split never receives hidden imputation truth. The seq10
variants use `sequential = TRUE, nseq = 10`; vimpute's convergence rule may
stop earlier. Fine-tuning occurs afresh at each fitted iteration.

Both kNN variants use five neighbours, range-standardized Gower distances,
median numeric aggregation and modal categorical aggregation. Each target
uses the original incomplete table, all other columns as distance variables,
and `useImputedDist = FALSE`. `kNN_rf` adds `weights = "auto"`: target-specific
random-forest impurity importances fitted on complete cases provide the
predictor weights (ranger's default 500 trees). This does not add RF predictions
as distance variables. Plain `kNN` uses equal predictor weights.

`cellwise` uses cell-weighted MM regression for numeric targets and weighted
multinomial classification for factors, with training-based predictor screening.
`cellwise_seq10` uses the same learners for up to ten sequential passes; it may
stop early at convergence. Both disable PMM and residual noise (`uncert = "none"`)
to match this benchmark's other vimpute variants, so donor screening is inactive.
Categorical imputation chooses the most probable class. Predictor categories
present in the task schema but absent from a target's observed training rows
are mapped internally to that predictor's training mode; supplied observed
cells remain unchanged.

The 20-repetition experiment recomputes all methods with the same configuration
and shared seed schedule. Earlier caches are preserved separately. Runtime
comparisons describe this hardware and execution environment and should not be
interpreted as universal speed ratios.

Ranger uses 100 trees and XGBoost 30 boosting rounds.
The seq10 variants use up to ten sequential passes; the other ranger/XGBoost variants
predict each target from the same incomplete table. TabICL's pretrained budget
differs from these fitted models, so this is a practical method comparison.

The full run is checkpointed by complete scenario. Restarting with the same
configuration and `resume = TRUE` retains completed scenarios and reruns an
interrupted scenario. Use a separate output directory when changing the
configuration. The report can be regenerated with `VIM_TABICL2_CACHED_ONLY=true`
and `VIM_TABICL2_RESULTS` set to a completed output directory without initializing
Python.

Python >= 3.10 and the optional R package `reticulate` are required for TabICL.
Install dependencies explicitly in the environment selected by reticulate:

```{bash installation, eval=FALSE}
python -m pip install -r inst/python/tabicl-requirements.txt
```

```{r regenerate, eval=FALSE}
# From the repository root, with the current VIM installed and the Python
# environment selected via RETICULATE_PYTHON:
# Rscript vignettes/precompute.R tabiclImpute-benchmark
# Edit config in tabiclImpute-benchmark.Rmd.orig for a different experiment.
# Set VIM_TABICL2_RESULTS to a fresh local directory if configuration changes.
# To render the already cached document only:
rmarkdown::render("vignettes/tabiclImpute-benchmark.Rmd")
```

## Datasets and preprocessing

* [Adult / Census Income](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/2/adult),
  Becker and Kohavi (1996), [DOI:10.24432/C5XW20](https://doi.org/10.24432/C5XW20):
  combine `adult.data` and `adult.test` (48,842 rows). Skip the test-file header,
  trim whitespace, remove the final period from test income labels, and parse
  `?` as missing. Combining the published splits is intentional: this is
  transductive reconstruction of missing cells, not a supervised income test.
* [Bank Marketing](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/222/bank+marketing),
  Moro, Rita and Cortez (2014), [DOI:10.24432/C5K306](https://doi.org/10.24432/C5K306):
  use `bank-full.csv` (45,211 rows), not the smaller sample or the different
  `bank-additional-full.csv`. Keep `unknown` as an observed category. It encodes
  recorded lack of knowledge; equating it with an artificially deleted value
  would change the evaluation target. Keep `pdays = -1` (no prior contact) as
  a structural sentinel in its original numeric column. Do not interpret it
  as a literal negative duration.

Both UCI datasets are distributed under CC BY 4.0. The script stores archive
URLs and MD5 checksums for provenance. Raw records are downloaded only with
explicit `download = TRUE` and are not bundled with VIM.

All character columns become unordered factors and all numeric measurements
remain numeric. No clipping, logarithms, balancing, rare-level pooling or
one-hot conversion is applied in shared preprocessing. Adult's `fnlwgt` remains
a predictor, not a survey weight; these are unweighted sample diagnostics.
Both education representations are retained. Bank's call duration is retained:
it is available for retrospective imputation, but would be inappropriate in a
pre-call subscription prediction task.

Remove rows with any existing NA to obtain complete references. Consequently,
Adult results describe its complete-case population and need not transfer to
people with naturally missing occupation or workclass. No correctness claim
is made for the unobserved true values in those removed rows.

```{r preprocessing, echo=FALSE}
audit <- read.csv(file.path(result_dir, "preprocessing.csv"))
knitr::kable(unique(audit[c("dataset", "rows", "complete_rows")]),
             caption = "Rows before and after complete-case selection")
knitr::kable(audit[audit$existing_na > 0 | audit$unknown > 0,
                   c("dataset", "variable", "existing_na", "unknown")],
             caption = "Existing missing values and retained unknown categories")
```

## Missingness simulation

Each repetition draws a new simple random sample without replacement from each
complete-case dataset at **`r paste(format(config$n, big.mark = ","), collapse = ", ")` rows**,
with **`r config$repetitions` repetition(s)** per scenario.
The seed schedule is shared across sizes, so samples overlap and sample-size
comparisons are not independent replications. The sampling algorithm may change
with the requested size; exact nesting is not assumed. All methods share the
same sample and mask within each dataset/size/repetition/mechanism/rate scenario.
The column `n` records the number of rows in every result table. Seeds and
training budgets are unchanged across sizes: more rows imply more observed context and, when fine-tuning is enabled, more training batches.
The targets are Adult `hours_per_week` and `income`, and Bank `balance` and `y`.
Other columns stay observed, including the MAR driver `age`.

`makeMissing()` selects exactly `round(rate * nrow(truth))` cells per
target, without replacement. MCAR uses uniform sampling. MAR uses
`weights = c(age = 1)` explicitly: the sampling weights are the logistic
transform of standardized observed age. These weights are **not** marginal
cell-deletion probabilities; the requested number of rows is sampled using
those relative weights. Higher age favours selection. Age is never masked,
and no target's hidden value drives another target's missingness. Using the
default MAR drivers instead could include the other incomplete numeric target,
so specifying the observed driver matters here.

The `where` attribute supplies the common mask for every competing method
and for scoring. We retain the original reference separately. With 250 rows,
the three requested rates correspond to 25, 50 and 100 masked cells in each
target. With other sample sizes the fractions may differ slightly due to
rounding. Masks are never redrawn to remove difficult cases.

```{r masks, eval=FALSE}
prepared <- uci_prepare("uci-cache", download = FALSE)
set.seed(config$seed)
truth <- prepared$data$Adult[sample(nrow(prepared$data$Adult), config$n[1L]), ]
incomplete <- makeMissing(truth, prop = .2, mechanism = "MAR",
  vars = c("hours_per_week", "income"), weights = c(age = 1), seed = config$seed)
mask <- attr(incomplete, "where")
incomplete <- droplevels(incomplete) # no levels learned solely from masked values
# truth is passed only to evaluation, never to a learner.
```

```{r realized-rates, echo=FALSE}
mask_rates <- unique(metrics[c("n", "dataset", "repetition", "mechanism", "rate",
                               "variable", "realized_rate")])
knitr::kable(aggregate(realized_rate ~ n + dataset + mechanism + rate + variable,
                       mask_rates, mean), digits = 3,
             caption = "Mean realized target-specific missing fractions")
```

## Evaluation

The pointwise metrics reuse VIM's existing tools and score artificially
masked cells only:

* `evaluation()` supplies numeric mean squared error (`err_num`) and the
  fraction of incorrect categories (`err_cat`). We report RMSE as
  `sqrt(err_num)`, misclassification as `err_cat`, and accuracy as
  `1 - err_cat`. Because the current implementation of `evaluation()` sums
  errors over the supplied table, the runner **subsets its inputs to masked
  cells for each target** and passes an all-TRUE `where` matrix. This makes
  the scoring boundary explicit. Whole-table calls are equivalent only when
  observed values are preserved, which the runner also checks.
* `nrmse(x, y, m = mask)` supplies **NRMSE**, using the standard deviation of
  the **masked true values**, as implemented by VIM. This is the convention
  used by the companion benchmark. Fewer than two masked values or zero
  masked-reference variance gives an undefined score, recorded as NA.
* **NRMSE_ref** (`nrmse_reference` in the CSV) retains the alternative
  `RMSE / sd(full sampled reference column)` requested for this UCI
  benchmark. It is separate from VIM's masked-reference NRMSE; under MAR the
  two denominators can differ substantially. Both denominators are common
  across methods within a scenario and are used only for evaluation.
* MAE, whole-table marginal distances and relationship diagnostics supplement
  these existing tools; they are not available from `evaluation()` itself.

## Results

The saved run contains `r nrow(runs)` method/scenario attempts,
`r sum(runs$status == "ok")` successful completions and
`r sum(runs$status != "ok")` recorded failures. Every method in a scenario
uses the same `makeMissing()` mask. A successful completion means that the
completed table passed schema, observed-value and finite-score checks; it does
not imply optimizer convergence. Numerical warnings are retained in `runs.csv`.
The warning summary below makes nonconverged fits and explicit fallbacks visible.

### Summary

```{r overview-values, include=FALSE}
num_summary <- aggregate(nrmse_reference ~ dataset + method,
                         subset(metrics, type == "numeric"), mean)
cat_summary <- aggregate(accuracy ~ dataset + method,
                         subset(metrics, type == "categorical"), mean)
time_summary <- aggregate(elapsed ~ method, runs, median)
overview <- data.frame(Method = config$methods)
for (ds in c("Adult", "Bank")) {
  a <- subset(num_summary, dataset == ds)
  b <- subset(cat_summary, dataset == ds)
  overview[[paste(ds, "numeric error")]] <- sprintf("%.3f", a$nrmse_reference[match(overview$Method, a$method)])
  overview[[paste(ds, "accuracy")]] <- sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * b$accuracy[match(overview$Method, b$method)])
}
med_seconds <- function(method) sprintf("%.3f", time_summary$elapsed[match(method, time_summary$method)])
```

The following overview averages equally across the saved sample sizes,
missingness mechanisms, missingness rates and repetitions. Numeric error is
RMSE divided by the complete target's standard deviation; lower is better.
Categorical accuracy is measured on hidden cells; higher is better. This table
summarizes **`r config$repetitions` repetitions**, not independent datasets.

```{r overview-table, echo=FALSE}
knitr::kable(overview, row.names = FALSE)
```

Across 20 repetitions, TabICL2 has the lowest average numeric errors among these
methods and the highest or essentially tied highest categorical accuracies.
Basic TabICL2 achieves normalized numeric errors of 0.899 on Adult and 0.991 on
Bank, with categorical accuracies of 82.4% and 90.0%. Fine-tuning gives only tiny
average numeric improvements (0.899 and 0.989); sequential TabICL2 increases
numeric error on both datasets. These are descriptive comparisons, not claims
of statistical significance.

RF weighting gives kNN modest improvements: Adult numeric error falls from
0.986 to 0.980 and Bank from 1.042 to 1.040. Adult categorical accuracy remains
about 78.3%, while Bank accuracy increases from 88.7% to 89.1%. Median runtime
increases from `r med_seconds("kNN")` to `r med_seconds("kNN_rf")` seconds.

Cellwise exhibits serious numeric instability. Single-pass Cellwise has a
normalized error of approximately 4.19e11 in one Adult scenario with 2,000 rows
and 40% missingness, driving its overall Adult mean to approximately 8.72e8.
Thus instability is not confined to the smallest sample size. Sequential
Cellwise avoids that particular extreme but still reaches about 900 at 250
rows; its overall Adult mean is 3.686. Restricting sequential Cellwise to at
least 500 rows gives an Adult mean of 0.991, still above kNN and TabICL2.
Bank numeric errors average 1.226 and 1.162 for single-pass and sequential
Cellwise. These extreme values are retained in all summaries and plots.

Categorical Cellwise also trails kNN on both datasets in the expanded experiment:
its single-pass accuracies are 77.7% on Adult and 86.5% on Bank, versus 78.3%
and 88.7% for kNN. Sequential Cellwise changes these to 77.6% and 86.6%.
Convergence warnings occur in 298 of 960 single-pass and 599 of 960 sequential
Cellwise runs. All methods return valid completed tables, and no featureless
fallbacks are recorded; validity does not establish numerical stability or
optimizer convergence.

Ranger offers a faster alternative with Adult accuracy of 81.7% (82.0%
sequentially) and Bank accuracy of 89.4% (89.5% sequentially). Sequential XGBoost
improves Adult numeric error from 0.925 to 0.914, but does not improve Bank
numeric error on average. Basic TabICL2 takes a median
`r med_seconds("vimpute_tabicl2")` seconds, compared with
`r med_seconds("vimpute_tabicl2_finetune")` seconds with fine-tuning,
`r med_seconds("vimpute_tabicl2_seq10")` seconds sequentially, and
`r med_seconds("vimpute_tabicl2_finetune_seq10")` seconds with both.
The additional TabICL2 computation provides little average accuracy benefit
under these training budgets. Timings span multiple resumed execution sessions.

The experiment uses two datasets and does not inject cellwise contamination.
It evaluates ordinary imputation performance, not contamination robustness.
Accuracy alone can conceal poor minority-class performance, and averages can
conceal unstable individual fits. The detailed scenario tables, warnings and
distribution diagnostics should therefore be read alongside the overview.

```{r status, echo=FALSE}
knitr::kable(as.data.frame(table(runs$n, runs$dataset, runs$method, runs$status)),
             col.names = c("Rows", "Dataset", "Method", "Status", "Runs"))
failed <- runs[runs$status != "ok", c("n", "dataset", "repetition", "mechanism", "rate", "method", "error")]
if (nrow(failed)) knitr::kable(failed)
```

```{r warning-summary, echo=FALSE}
warning_text <- ifelse(is.na(runs$warnings), "", runs$warnings)
warning_counts <- aggregate(cbind(
  warned = as.integer(nzchar(warning_text)),
  convergence_warning = as.integer(grepl("converg", warning_text, ignore.case = TRUE)),
  fallback_warning = as.integer(grepl("fallback|falling back", warning_text, ignore.case = TRUE))) ~
  method, data = runs, FUN = sum)
knitr::kable(warning_counts, row.names = FALSE,
  caption = "Runs with recorded warnings (counts may overlap).")
```

The following tables retain number of rows, dataset, mechanism and nominal rate. Scores are
means across successful repetitions, with their sample standard deviations in
parentheses. Counts are shown so failures cannot improve a ranking invisibly.
The saved configuration has `r config$repetitions` repetition(s). With one
repetition, standard deviations are unavailable; with few repetitions they
describe variation only roughly. They are not confidence intervals or significance tests. Methods are paired by
sample and mask, so formal comparisons would require paired analysis that accounts for the shared
samples and masks. Empty cells or `NA` indicate unavailable metrics: numeric error
measures do not apply to categorical targets, classification accuracy does not
apply to numeric targets, and a standard deviation requires at least two
successful repetitions. These are missing summary statistics, not unfilled
imputation cells. Auxiliary hurdle-model `*_zero_prob` diagnostics are excluded
from the scored data; actual target columns must contain no missing values. Success counts use completed
attempts as the denominator. The Coverage column distinguishes pending requested
repetitions from scenarios outside the saved experiment.

```{r result-tables, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}
fmt <- function(x) {
  x <- x[is.finite(x)]
  if (!length(x)) return("—")
  sprintf("%.3f (%.3f)", mean(x), if (length(x) > 1) sd(x) else NA_real_)
}
for (nr in config$n) for (ds in unique(runs$dataset)) for (mech in unique(runs$mechanism)) {
  cat("\n\n### ", ds, ": ", mech, ", ", nr, " rows\n\n", sep = "")
  tab <- do.call(rbind, lapply(config$rates, function(rate) {
    do.call(rbind, lapply(config$methods, function(method) {
      x <- metrics[metrics$n == nr & metrics$dataset == ds & metrics$mechanism == mech &
                     metrics$rate == rate & metrics$method == method, ]
      rr <- runs[runs$n == nr & runs$dataset == ds & runs$mechanism == mech &
                   runs$rate == rate & runs$method == method, ]
      requested <- config$repetitions
      pending <- max(0L, requested - nrow(rr))
      data.frame(Rate = rate, Method = method,
        Coverage = if (!requested) "Not requested" else if (pending)
          paste(pending, "pending") else "Complete",
        Success = paste(sum(rr$status == "ok"), nrow(rr), sep = "/"),
        RMSE = fmt(x$rmse), MAE = fmt(x$mae), NRMSE = fmt(x$nrmse), NRMSE_ref = fmt(x$nrmse_reference),
        Accuracy = fmt(x$accuracy), Misclassification = fmt(x$misclassification),
        Seconds = fmt(rr$elapsed[rr$status == "ok"]))
    }))
  }))
  print(knitr::kable(tab, row.names = FALSE))
  cat("\n\n")
}
```

```{r error-plot, echo=FALSE, eval=have_results && has_ggplot, fig.height=12}
library(ggplot2)
numeric_scores <- subset(metrics, type == "numeric")
ggplot(numeric_scores, aes(factor(rate), nrmse, colour = method, group = method)) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "line",
    data = function(d) d[d$method %in% multi_rate_methods, ]) +
  geom_point(position = position_dodge(width = .25), alpha = .65) +
  facet_grid(n + dataset ~ mechanism, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_y_continuous(trans = "log1p",
    breaks = function(limits) expm1(seq(log1p(max(0, limits[1])), log1p(limits[2]), length.out = 4)),
    labels = function(x) format(signif(x, 3), trim = TRUE)) +
  labs(x = "Nominal missing fraction", y = "VIM masked-reference NRMSE (log1p scale)",
       colour = "Method", caption = "Points: repetitions; lines: means. Lower is better.") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + guides(colour = guide_legend(ncol = 3))
```

```{r categorical-plot, echo=FALSE, eval=have_results && has_ggplot, fig.height=12}
ggplot(subset(metrics, type == "categorical"),
       aes(factor(rate), accuracy, colour = method, group = method)) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "line",
    data = function(d) d[d$method %in% multi_rate_methods, ]) +
  geom_point(position = position_dodge(width = .25), alpha = .65) +
  facet_grid(n + dataset ~ mechanism) + ylim(0, 1) +
  labs(x = "Nominal missing fraction", y = "Masked-cell accuracy", colour = "Method") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + guides(colour = guide_legend(ncol = 3))
```

```{r runtime-plot, echo=FALSE, eval=have_results && has_ggplot, fig.height=18}
ggplot(subset(runs, status == "ok"), aes(method, elapsed, colour = factor(rate))) +
  geom_point(position = position_jitter(width = .15, height = 0, seed = 1)) +
  facet_grid(n + dataset ~ mechanism) + scale_y_log10() + coord_flip() +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Seconds for both targets (log scale)", colour = "Missing fraction") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + guides(colour = guide_legend(ncol = 3))
```

```{r structure-plot, echo=FALSE, eval=have_results && has_ggplot, fig.height=18}
ggplot(metrics, aes(factor(rate), marginal_distance, colour = method, group = method)) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "line",
    data = function(d) d[d$method %in% multi_rate_methods, ]) +
  stat_summary(fun = mean, geom = "point") +
  facet_grid(n + dataset + type ~ mechanism) +
  labs(x = "Nominal missing fraction", y = "Whole-table marginal distance",
       colour = "Method", caption = "Numeric: ECDF distance; categorical: total variation. Lower is better.") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + guides(colour = guide_legend(ncol = 3))
```

